From: NFCX maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] add high-performance (-H) and x64 (-X) Nested modes
Upstream-Status: Pending

The Nested attack spends nearly all of its wall-clock time in
lfsr_recovery32(), which is called once for every nonce inside the
median +- tolerance window. Every one of those calls is independent, so the
window can be scanned by several threads and the candidate keys merged
afterwards.

This patch adds two runtime modes to mfoc:

  -H  high-performance mode: the probe window is scanned by one worker per CPU
      core (NFCX_MFOC_THREADS overrides the worker count). The recovered
      candidate set is identical to the serial scan; only the ordering of the
      candidates differs.

  -X  x64 mode: the three-bit nonce parity check is evaluated for all three
      byte lanes inside one 64-bit expression using __builtin_parity(), which
      maps to a hardware popcount on 64-bit targets. Rejected at startup on
      32-bit builds.

Behaviour without -H/-X is unchanged: the serial scanner runs the same
lfsr_recovery32()/lfsr_rollback_word()/crypto1_get_lfsr() sequence as before.

tests/nfcx-perf-test.c (built by "make check") cross-checks the wide parity
check against the upstream valid_nonce() formula, verifies that the parallel
scanner recovers exactly the same candidates as the serial scanner, and reports
the measured speed-up.

